	VAX to PDP-12 and PDP-12 to VAX communications.

I.  At the VAX, communication is implemented by programs PDPCOM and
PIP, which are found in the library [TELLER.DAVID].
The communications programs can handle A-Z, 0-9 and special characters
that are used in Fortran programs, but will not handle control chars,
lower case, or other weird characters.  Use PIP/T to expand tabs.

II.  PDP-12 to VAX communication is accomplished by the VAXCM2
program and PIP.  The information to be transmitted should be put in a file
with a six character filename and .DA extension.  You will need to 
know the number of lines in the file.  Lines may not be longer than
80 characters.  Then proceed as follows:
	Turn on VADIC modem and check wire to PDP-12.
	.R VAXCM2  and give it filename and number of lines.
	Call VAX and login.

	$MCR PIP   (this calls PIP from the system library)
	PIP>fname.ext=TTA5:   (the 1200 baud line is terminal 5)

	Wait for 'CR', then hit ^G to start transmission.  MQ
shows characters being transmitted.  Wait for ^Z message from PIP.
Hit ^Y to exit PIP on the VAX. Logout, and hit ^C to terminate
PDP-12 program.
	Note that if the VAX is very busy, the file may be incomplete,
as the VAX doesnt know that the lines continue to come.

III.  VAX to PDP-12 communication uses  the VAX12 and PDPCOM programs. 
Only 80 characters per line can be transmitted.  At the PDP-12, the file
will be written on DSK: with your specified six character filename and
.DA extension.
Proceed as follows:
	Turn modem on.
	.R VAX12  and give it the filename on the PDP-12.
	Call VAX and login.
	Setup the file PDPCOM.COM as follows:
		$Deass/all
		$As TTA5: FOR004
		$As name.ext FOR005	/this is the file to be sent
		$Run PDPCOM
		$Exit
	When done with this editing (*EB to get out of ED) and the
	$sign appears, type @PDPCOM to run the program in immediate mode.
	At the end of the program, the PDP-12 tells the # of lines
and the # of characters received.  The # of lines is 1 less than the
VAX told you initially it would send.  Control then returns to the VAX
which prints the number of characters it sent.  The number should
of characters should agree between ths machines in a successful file
transfer.
	$ symbols will not be sent as these are used to terminate trans-
mission.  They are replaced by % signs in the file on the PDP-12.  Use
SCROLL to restore them to $ signs.  The number of signs converted is
told to you by the VAX.
	Logout, hit ^G to terminate PDP-12 program.
	VAX12.FT uses the subroutines GCHAR.SB and VACTV.SB; the latter
is a slightly modified version of IACTV.SB.
	The program PDPCOM.FOR on the VAX has several delay loops
to allow the PDP-12 time to write the lines.  These delay loops may
have to be changed from time to time if the characteristics of either
computer change.

IV.  Login procedure on VAX:
	Turn VADIC modem on (switch at rear).
	Call VAX (5-7572), wait for high pitch tone, pull up
	white knob on phone.
	Hit 'CR' and wait for:

	Username: TELLER
	Password: DAVID    (will not echo)
	$

V.  Notes on using the VAX and other common problems--
When typing commands to the VAX delete and ^U may be used (they work
but do not echo).  ^Y aborts whatever the VAX is currently doing.

VI.  If you want to use the PDP-12 as a terminal only, use either
VAXCM2 or VAX12, giving a dummy file name at the beginning of the 
program.  Just never hit ^G.

